DevSecOps just means adding a few scanners to CI/CD?

You're probably doing it wrong.

Here’s what real DevSecOps actually looks like 👇
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
🔴 ANTI-PATTERNS
→ Secrets committed to Git
 → Containers running as root
 → *:* IAM permissions
 → Terraform applied without scanning
 → Kubernetes deployed with default RBAC
 → CI/CD that reports vulnerabilities but never blocks them

Result:
 Silent risk.
 Security theatre.
 Future incidents waiting to happen.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
🟢 BEST PRACTICES
1️⃣ Shift Left for real
 Security starts at commit time.
 Pre-commit hooks. Secret scanning. Protected branches.
2️⃣ Enforce least privilege
 IAM, Git access, Kubernetes RBAC — no broad permissions.
3️⃣ Secure containers by default
 Non-root users. Minimal images. Runtime restrictions.
4️⃣ Scan infrastructure before provisioning
 Terraform & Kubernetes manifests must be validated before apply.
5️⃣ CI/CD as a security gate
 Fail builds on critical vulnerabilities.
 No exceptions without review.
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

📊 THE REALITY
• Most breaches start with misconfiguration
 • Most misconfigurations are preventable
 • Prevention costs far less than incident response

DevSecOps isn’t about tools.
 It’s about disciplined engineering decisions.

Security isn’t a phase.
 It’s a responsibility.
